what  is transfer learning  the idea of transfer learning is to  leverage the knowledge required by a  model train with lots of data  on another task the model a will be  trained specifically for task a  now let's say you want to train model b  for a different task  one option would be to train the model  from scratch  this could take lots of computation time  and data  instead we could initialize model b with  the same weights as model a  transferring the knowledge of model a on  task b  when training from scratch all the  model's weights are initialized randomly  in this example we are training a belt  model on the task of recognizing  if two sentences are similar or not  on the left it's trained from scratch  and on the right  it's fine tuning a pre-trained model as  we can see  using transfer learning on the  pre-trained model yields better results  and it doesn't matter if you train  longer the training from scratch is kept  around 70  accuracy or the retrain model beats the  86 percent easily  this is because pre-trained models are  usually trained on large amounts of data  but provides a model with a statistical  understanding of the language used  during pre-training  in computer vision transfer learning has  been applied successfully  for almost 10 years models are  frequently retrained on imagenet  a dataset containing 1.2 millions of  photo images  each image is classified by one of 1000  levels  training like this on labeled data is  called  supervised learning in natural language  processing  transfer learning is a bit more recent a  key difference with imagenet  is that the training is usually  self-supervised which means it doesn't  require us imagination for the labels  a very common pre-training objective is  to guess the next word in a sentence  which only requires lots and lots of  text gpd2 for instance  was retrained this way using the content  of 45 million links posted by users  on reddit another example of  self-supervised per training objective  is to predict the value of randomly  masked words which is similar to feeling  less pronounced tests you may have done  in school  but was retrained this way using the  english wikipedia  and 11 000 in published books  in practice transfer learning is applied  on a given model  by throwing away its head that is its  last layers focused on the  training objective and replacing it with  a new  randomly initialized ad suitable for the  task attempt  for instance when we invited you in the  birth model earlier  removes the advert classified mask words  and replaced it with a classifier with  two outputs  since our tasks add two levels to be as  efficient as possible  the pre-trained model used should be as  similar as possible to the task it's  fine tuned on  for instance if the problem is to  classify german sentences  it's best to use the german pre-trained  model  but with the good comes the bad the  pre-trained model does not only transfer  its knowledge  but also any bias it may contain  imagenet mostly contains images coming  from the united states and western  europe  the models fine tuned with it usually  will perform better on images from these  countries  open ai also studied the bias in the  prediction of its gpt3 model  which was between using the guest and  x-word objectives  changing the gender of the prompt from  e-wisebury to  she was very changed the predictions for  mostly neutral objectives  to almost only physical ones in the  model card of the gbt2 model  openai also acknowledges its bias and  discourages its use  in systems that interact with humans

